---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How Speed, Distance and Time Are Related - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "The formula linking speed, distance and time, how to rearrange it, and how to keep the units consistent.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-speed-distance-and-time-are-related.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Maths basics</p>
    <h1>How Speed, Distance and Time Are Related</h1>
    <p class="lead">Speed, distance and time are linked by one simple formula. If you know any two, you can work out the third.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The formula</h2>
<p>Distance = speed × time. Speed = distance ÷ time. Time = distance ÷ speed.</p>
<h2>An example</h2>
<p>A car travelling at 60 km per hour for 2 hours covers 60 × 2 = 120 km. If it covers 120 km at 60 km per hour, it takes 120 ÷ 60 = 2 hours.</p>
<h2>Keep units consistent</h2>
<p>If speed is in kilometres per hour, time must be in hours and distance comes out in kilometres. Minutes need converting first: 30 minutes is 0.5 hours.</p>
<h2>Average speed</h2>
<p>Average speed is total distance divided by total time, not the average of the speeds. Driving 60 km at 60 km/h and 60 km at 30 km/h gives an average of 120 ÷ 3 = 40 km/h.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/speed-distance-time-calculator.html">Speed, Distance and Time Calculator</a> fills in the missing value when you give it the other two.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
</Base>
